In this lesson we'll look at how four little letters completely change the shape of a sine, cosine, or tangent graph. Think of the base function first, and then we'll layer the changes on top. The letter a gives you a vertical stretch by its absolute value, which for trig we call the amplitude. If a is negative, the whole graph flips upside down across the x-axis. Next comes n, which squeezes or stretches the graph sideways by a factor of one over n, and that's what makes the period shorter or longer. Then there's b hiding inside the bracket. Because of a little algebra quirk, x plus b actually shifts the graph to the left by b, not the right, so keep your eyes open for that sign flip. Finally, c just slides the whole graph up or down, and for sine and cosine it tells you the centre line that the wave is wobbling around. Put it all together and you can read any transformed trig graph like a sentence. Now let's see it in action.